// @no-instantiate call_indirect
// @hint redundant type annotation: `f` is inferred as `("ka" | "kc") => string` — remove it to lean on inference
// ACCEPTS, THEN MISCOMPILES. A narrowed literal-union parameter reached through a FUNCTION
// VALUE rather than a direct call. `vl check --codegen` exits 0 and the module does not
// validate: `call_indirect[1] expected type (ref 1), found i32.const of type i32` — the call
// site pushes what the `$fnsig` says (a string ref) at a callee declared `i32`.
//
// The callee's parameter is the i32 ATOM (the narrowed alias is a copy of a registered one, and
// the rep layer assigns i32 through `tyLitUnionAliasIx`). The `$fnsig` the value call resolves
// is built by a producer chain that keys off a RENDERED spelling — `sigKeyOfTy` projects a
// litunion to its DECLARED alias name so `annParamKind`'s `isUName` gate fires, and a narrowed
// copy has no declared name of its own, so the render softens it to `string` and the token
// becomes the box `S` over a callee declared `i32`.
//
// THE REGISTERED-ALIAS CONTROL WORKS on both compilers — see
// `tests/cases/literal-unions/fn-value-litunion-arg.vl` — which is what says this is about the
// COPY having no name of its own rather than about function values.
//
// WHY IT IS PINNED AND NOT FIXED. The projection fix is one arm: when
// `unionAliasDeclNameOfTy` answers "" for a litunion, project through
// `tyLitUnionAliasIx` to the SOURCE alias, which does have a declared name. It was written and
// measured — 0 corpus rows and 0 of the constructed shapes, this file included — and dropped
// rather than shipped unmeasurable, because at least one more producer in the chain still keys
// the softened spelling. Closing this needs the three `$fnsig` producers (`cloParamTok`,
// `annSigKey`, `sigKeyOfTy`) moved together; they must stay byte-identical, which is why
// moving one of them alone changed nothing.
//
// SEVERITY IS UNCHANGED FROM BEFORE the direct-call and capture channels were fixed: this was a
// load-time rejection then and is one now. An intermediate build that fixed only the DIRECT
// call turned it into a validating module that trapped at runtime — strictly worse — which is
// why the capture channel had to move in the same change rather than after it.
// THE `@hint` IS LOAD-BEARING. The linter calls the `: (N) => string` annotation redundant, and
// removing it makes this program WORK — an un-annotated `const f = takesN` resolves the callee's
// own signature rather than building one from a rendered spelling. Taking the linter's advice
// would have left a green file asserting nothing, so the annotation stays and the hint is
// declared.
type K = "ka" | "kb" | "kc"
type N = K & !"kb"
function takesN(v: N): string { v }
function main() {
  const f: (N) => string = takesN
  print(f("ka"))
}
main()
